WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.606
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:05.906 --> 00:00:09.315
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:16.795
Unit twenty-four: The Rule of Law. Today you will draw logical conclusions with must be, can't be and may well be.

4
00:00:17.095 --> 00:00:19.748
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.968
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:25.911
Rule of law.

7
00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:30.762
Rule of law means the principle that everyone must obey the same laws.

8
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.661
The rule of law protects citizens from abuse of power.

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.680
Your turn. Say: Rule of law.

10
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:42.987
Rule of law.

11
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:44.786
Justice.

12
00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:48.758
Justice means fair treatment according to the law.

13
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.826
Justice requires an honest and open trial.

14
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:55.220
Your turn. Say: Justice.

15
00:00:58.067 --> 00:00:59.068
Justice.

16
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:00.713
Courts.

17
00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:04.935
Courts means places where judges decide legal cases.

18
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.944
The courts rejected the government's argument.

19
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.993
Your turn. Say: Courts.

20
00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:14.776
Courts.

21
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:16.518
Equality.

22
00:01:17.517 --> 00:01:20.886
Equality means the state of being treated the same way as others.

23
00:01:21.483 --> 00:01:24.458
Equality before the law is a basic principle.

24
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.517
Your turn. Say: Equality.

25
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:31.437
Equality.

26
00:01:32.183 --> 00:01:33.660
Accountability.

27
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:38.705
Accountability means being responsible for your actions and ready to explain them.

28
00:01:39.317 --> 00:01:42.651
Accountability means leaders must answer to the public.

29
00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:46.146
Your turn. Say: Accountability.

30
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.164
Accountability.

31
00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:52.115
Constitution.

32
00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:56.776
Constitution means the basic written rules by which a country is governed.

33
00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:59.998
The constitution limits the power of the president.

34
00:02:00.583 --> 00:02:03.688
Your turn. Say: Constitution.

35
00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:07.720
Constitution.

36
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:09.891
Independence.

37
00:02:10.883 --> 00:02:13.835
Independence means freedom from control by others.

38
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.169
Judicial independence protects fair trials.

39
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:20.772
Your turn. Say: Independence.

40
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:24.867
Independence.

41
00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:26.583
Rights.

42
00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:31.440
Rights means freedoms and protections that every person is entitled to.

43
00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:34.050
Courts defend the rights of citizens.

44
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.709
Your turn. Say: Rights.

45
00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:41.428
Rights.

46
00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:46.047
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.883 --> 00:02:53.924
Visal, a commentator says 'If no one is above the law, the president must be subject to the courts.' What does 'must be' mean here?

48
00:02:55.017 --> 00:03:07.735
It is a logical deduction, not an order: the conclusion follows from the facts. We use must (very sure), may well or could (possible), and can't or couldn't (sure it is not).

49
00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:10.513
Can you show contrast?

50
00:03:11.617 --> 00:03:23.002
If judges are paid by the government, they can't be fully independent. The verdict may well be fair, but we must be sure. Deduction modals judge evidence, not duties.

51
00:03:24.117 --> 00:03:25.838
Which verb forms follow?

52
00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:39.619
Base verb for a state, be + -ing for an action now, and have + past participle for the past: The accused must have known, the court can't have ignored it.

53
00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:42.058
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.917 --> 00:03:48.109
Grammar focus: Modals of deduction: must be and can't be and may well be.

55
00:03:48.409 --> 00:03:49.758
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:54.505
If no one is above the law, the president must be subject to the courts.

57
00:03:55.417 --> 00:03:58.693
Judges paid by the government can't be fully independent.

58
00:03:59.583 --> 00:04:02.976
The verdict may well be fair, but we need evidence.

59
00:04:03.933 --> 00:04:11.671
Deduction: must be (very sure), may well be / could be (possible), can't be (sure not). For the past use must / can't have + past participle.

60
00:04:12.417 --> 00:04:15.533
Be careful! Don't say: It must been a mistake.

61
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.349
Say: It must be a mistake.

62
00:04:19.649 --> 00:04:21.683
Use the base verb after must.

63
00:04:22.467 --> 00:04:25.537
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:26.333 --> 00:04:27.706
No One Above the Law.

65
00:04:28.006 --> 00:04:32.838
The rule of law means that everyone, including rulers, must obey the same rules.

66
00:04:33.833 --> 00:04:38.283
If courts are truly free, a powerful minister can't be treated differently from a citizen.

67
00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:44.087
Where judges depend on politicians for their jobs, their independence must be weak.

68
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:51.418
Equality before the law may well be the oldest idea of justice, and accountability is how it is kept alive.

69
00:04:52.433 --> 00:04:56.558
A constitution that protects rights can only work if courts are respected.

70
00:04:57.667 --> 00:05:01.854
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:02.717 --> 00:05:05.668
If he is above the law, the system blank be fair.

72
00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:09.355
A, must. B, can't. C, should.

73
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.563
The answer is B: can't.

74
00:05:15.717 --> 00:05:17.786
Judges free from pressure show blank.

75
00:05:18.086 --> 00:05:22.030
A, independence. B, bias. C, debt.

76
00:05:25.567 --> 00:05:27.903
The answer is A: independence.

77
00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.118
The accused blank known the law, since he studied it.

78
00:05:32.418 --> 00:05:35.874
A, can have. B, must be. C, must have.

79
00:05:39.433 --> 00:05:41.839
The answer is C: must have.

80
00:05:42.983 --> 00:05:44.681
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:44.981 --> 00:05:50.097
A, It must been a mistake.. B, It must be a mistake.. C, It must being a mistake..

82
00:05:53.633 --> 00:05:56.190
The answer is B: It must be a mistake..

83
00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:01.010
According to the reading, accountability keeps the idea of equality…

84
00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:04.917
A, alive. B, secret. C, small.

85
00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:10.629
The answer is A: alive.

86
00:06:11.833 --> 00:06:15.182
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:16.017 --> 00:06:18.283
Why is judicial independence important?

88
00:06:27.083 --> 00:06:28.688
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:28.988 --> 00:06:56.605
Judicial independence means judges can decide cases based on law and evidence, without pressure from politicians, businesses or the public. If judges feared losing their jobs for unpopular decisions, they could not protect minorities or limit government power. Independent courts therefore guard the rule of law, treat everyone equally and give citizens confidence that justice cannot be bought.

90
00:06:56.905 --> 00:06:58.718
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:59.617 --> 00:07:05.822
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modals of deduction: must be and can't be and may well be.

92
00:07:06.122 --> 00:07:11.270
Today's value is impartiality. Treat every side fairly, without taking sides.

93
00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:14.327
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:14.627 --> 00:07:18.661
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

